WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.269 --> 00:00:06.539
Okey dokey, let's get started with today's video!

00:00:09.230 --> 00:00:11.540
Hey everyone, how's it going?

00:00:11.540 --> 00:00:12.579
Hope you are well.

00:00:13.100 --> 00:00:16.140
Today we're going to be talking about learning.

00:00:16.140 --> 00:00:17.799
The more you know.

00:00:17.990 --> 00:00:20.559
So questions I get often in my DM's are questions

00:00:20.560 --> 00:00:23.480
Like how do you stay up-to-date with new technologies?

00:00:23.480 --> 00:00:27.879
or how do you stay relevant with the tech industry when things are moving so fast?

00:00:28.310 --> 00:00:30.340
So we're gonna hit on three things today

00:00:30.590 --> 00:00:35.460
1. Whether it's true that you always need to stay up-to-date with new technologies to stay relevant.

00:00:35.460 --> 00:00:37.389
2. My own experiences with it

00:00:37.390 --> 00:00:43.930
And if it is true, and if it's not a myth, 3. how to go about doing it and how to think about it

00:00:44.059 --> 00:00:46.040
Let's get started

00:00:46.040 --> 00:00:50.859
So it's no secret that new technologies are coming out all the time

00:00:51.170 --> 00:00:54.340
When new technologies are always coming out it is kind of true

00:00:54.340 --> 00:00:57.339
that you do have to stay up to date with new technologies

00:00:57.620 --> 00:01:01.599
But it's also true that not everyone is an expert in every new technology

00:01:01.909 --> 00:01:06.339
And a lot of software engineers and most companies will not adopt these new

00:01:06.680 --> 00:01:10.599
technologies as soon as they come out or even two to five years out

00:01:11.150 --> 00:01:16.500
And personally, I think that one of the best and also scariest things about being a software engineer

00:01:16.500 --> 00:01:21.260
is precisely this. That you're always going to be learning as a software engineer,

00:01:21.260 --> 00:01:25.420
and I also want to note that always learning doesn't necessarily mean that you're always learning new

00:01:25.579 --> 00:01:28.089
Technologies, you're always just learning new skills

00:01:28.609 --> 00:01:30.789
And we're gonna touch on that in a little bit

00:01:31.070 --> 00:01:32.229
But before we do that

00:01:32.229 --> 00:01:36.360
I wanted to just kind of talk about almost the essence of software engineering

00:01:36.360 --> 00:01:39.639
and I think the essence is that you're always failing

00:01:39.640 --> 00:01:41.949
It's literally a career about failing

00:01:42.499 --> 00:01:47.300
Learning from your failures and gaining skills and doing better next time

00:01:47.300 --> 00:01:49.629
If you think about how software is always iterative

00:01:49.630 --> 00:01:54.189
I think it's just based on that precipice of you're always going to fail and you're always going to make mistakes

00:01:54.499 --> 00:01:58.449
But there's so much to learn from all of those mistakes that you can do better next time

00:01:58.450 --> 00:02:03.240
I think it's also based on the fact that there's nothing perfect in software engineering.

00:02:03.240 --> 00:02:04.780
There's no perfect line of code

00:02:04.780 --> 00:02:06.340
There's no perfect software engineer

00:02:06.340 --> 00:02:08.840
There's no perfect application or code base

00:02:08.840 --> 00:02:11.950
For those of you who do have experience diving into existing code bases

00:02:11.950 --> 00:02:15.280
you know that there's a lot of dust bunnies and

00:02:15.740 --> 00:02:21.819
Weird architectural decisions and just lots of cruft that exists. I know that whenever I start a new job

00:02:21.819 --> 00:02:23.390
I'm like "Oh, your app is great!"

00:02:23.390 --> 00:02:28.820
like "It's so stable and it's probably made so well" and then I peek behind the covers and I'm like

00:02:28.820 --> 00:02:35.500
"What is this mess? This makes no sense!" And I think you'll just find that at all the roles

00:02:35.640 --> 00:02:37.340
Nothing is perfect in software engineering

00:02:37.340 --> 00:02:42.560
so once you realize that nothing is perfect then also you realize that oh

00:02:42.560 --> 00:02:46.440
It's totally okay to make mistakes and fail and just learn from your failures

00:02:46.440 --> 00:02:48.830
because there's just so much to learn all the time

00:02:48.960 --> 00:02:53.629
Now it took a little bit for me to wrap my head around the fact that I'm always gonna be failing

00:02:53.640 --> 00:03:00.300
So I just want to take us back to like my first job as a software engineer where I was doing IOS development

00:03:00.820 --> 00:03:05.300
I hadn't really done any meaningful amount of iOS development before that

00:03:05.300 --> 00:03:10.620
like I made an app once with some people of which I wrote maybe a couple lines of code

00:03:11.220 --> 00:03:15.100
So I knew that in my first role, I would need to do a lot of learning

00:03:15.110 --> 00:03:19.639
I had no idea how to use Xcode. I barely wrote Objective C

00:03:19.709 --> 00:03:27.289
And so that's the thing that I really focused on in my first couple of years. So that's exactly what I did

00:03:27.290 --> 00:03:28.380
I told my manager

00:03:28.380 --> 00:03:28.880
Hey

00:03:28.880 --> 00:03:30.880
I know nothing

00:03:30.959 --> 00:03:35.940
please help me learn something so that I can be a valuable software engineer

00:03:35.940 --> 00:03:39.080
and contribute to the codebase and the team and the application.

00:03:39.080 --> 00:03:43.100
And I just really wanted to be exposed to lots of different parts of iOS development in

00:03:43.470 --> 00:03:47.600
All the different areas of software development, web development, security gaming development

00:03:47.600 --> 00:03:54.079
You can go so so deep into each area and within each area, there's lots of other little areas

00:03:54.080 --> 00:04:00.499
There's just the whole world to learn basically in each thing and that's exactly what my manager did

00:04:00.660 --> 00:04:06.529
he assigned me books to read, he told me some courses to take as well as some tutorials to do and

00:04:06.630 --> 00:04:10.850
Also, I found mentors within my team who could also guide me through all of this, too

00:04:10.850 --> 00:04:14.809
And I really can't emphasize enough how much I didn't know at the beginning

00:04:15.300 --> 00:04:21.830
But once I realized and told myself and told others and was honest with myself like "Yo, I don't know stuff!"

00:04:21.830 --> 00:04:28.879
That's where the learning began because I was willing to accept the fact that you know, I'm a failure right now

00:04:28.880 --> 00:04:35.450
But I can get myself out of this by learning new skills. And yes, the beginning was very very hard

00:04:35.690 --> 00:04:37.700
I remember my very first bug bash

00:04:37.700 --> 00:04:40.159
which is worse where we all sit together and just

00:04:40.590 --> 00:04:47.390
resolve a bunch of bugs and sitting there being like "I don't... I don't know how to do any of this cuz I

00:04:47.490 --> 00:04:51.049
don't know what I'm doing and I can barely read

00:04:51.270 --> 00:04:55.549
objective C" and always being faced with what little I knew was tough

00:04:55.860 --> 00:05:01.249
But those are the little failure points that I turned into motivation to learn new skills

00:05:01.530 --> 00:05:06.769
And then after a while maybe about six months to a year, I looked back and I was like, you know

00:05:06.770 --> 00:05:10.850
I actually know some stuff especially compared to my first week on the job

00:05:11.190 --> 00:05:16.219
And I'm really proud of myself for putting in that work and accepting the fact that you know

00:05:16.220 --> 00:05:20.059
I don't know a whole lot and I need to learn a lot more

00:05:20.130 --> 00:05:25.309
and now I do know a lot more and I feel way more competent as an engineer and I know a lot more about

00:05:25.500 --> 00:05:26.390
iOS development

00:05:26.390 --> 00:05:32.749
I mean a big part of this is just reaching out for help and asking people and telling people like "Yo, help me!"

00:05:32.750 --> 00:05:37.549
"I don't know what I'm doing!" So that was kind of my experience as an early career software engineer

00:05:37.550 --> 00:05:40.400
Just absorbing everything about the technology

00:05:40.400 --> 00:05:43.940
And now I'm gonna get to the point where I mentioned earlier about learning other

00:05:44.100 --> 00:05:46.369
Skills that are also really important

00:05:46.440 --> 00:05:51.350
So after I felt like I had a good grasp of iOS development, maybe that was about like two years later

00:05:51.350 --> 00:05:57.080
I realized that there aren't really other paved paths past this point

00:05:57.360 --> 00:06:02.720
it was all just about figuring out what I wanted to get better at, what I failed at, and

00:06:03.240 --> 00:06:09.530
Realizing from there the skills that I want and need in order to progress towards a place that I want to be

00:06:09.870 --> 00:06:12.240
Now this all sounds vague and ambiguous.

00:06:12.240 --> 00:06:15.799
But really what I'm trying to say is things like product skills, people skills

00:06:16.380 --> 00:06:18.380
creativity skills, writing skills

00:06:18.450 --> 00:06:21.200
These are all important skills to software engineering

00:06:21.540 --> 00:06:24.860
That I didn't realize we're important at the beginning

00:06:24.860 --> 00:06:28.549
And actually I would argue that in order to become a really well-rounded

00:06:28.650 --> 00:06:31.669
Software engineer, it's not just about the technical skills

00:06:31.669 --> 00:06:36.049
You need all of the other skills in order to do a good job. For instance,

00:06:36.050 --> 00:06:39.320
the people skills that I've worked on have enabled me to talk about technology to

00:06:39.560 --> 00:06:46.440
non-technical people and has really translated to like the content that I make on here, which I enjoy doing!

00:06:46.440 --> 00:06:52.020
I mean if we're being really honest, if you told me in my first year working that I would eventually make a

00:06:52.560 --> 00:06:54.859
YouTube channel about software engineering I'd be like

00:06:54.859 --> 00:06:56.400
"Whaaaaattttttt

00:06:56.400 --> 00:06:59.569
Nahh, that's not happening." But here we are!

00:07:00.419 --> 00:07:06.019
Because I mean I realized that I just want to do this and it would be fun. So here I am

00:07:06.270 --> 00:07:07.310
Thanks for subscribing

00:07:07.310 --> 00:07:09.349
If you're not subscribed click on the subscribe button

00:07:09.349 --> 00:07:15.289
And I realize at this point when there were all these other skills that I would need to learn to

00:07:15.569 --> 00:07:18.079
Be the best person and software engineer

00:07:18.080 --> 00:07:20.240
I could be, I realized that there's really a

00:07:20.550 --> 00:07:26.780
Merit to being a lifelong learner as a software engineer and I think that is the really important lesson here

00:07:27.090 --> 00:07:29.630
Yes, software engineering means you're always failing

00:07:29.880 --> 00:07:35.449
But in order to overcome that, you just have to be a lifelong learner and the best news is,

00:07:35.449 --> 00:07:40.129
anybody can be a lifelong learner. You just have to have that kind of like: I'm okay to fail

00:07:40.130 --> 00:07:43.849
I accept that I fail. I'm going to figure out how not to fail the next time and

00:07:44.729 --> 00:07:49.788
Understand what you need to learn to do better. So you might be asking what does this actually look like?

00:07:49.789 --> 00:07:56.119
Like how do software engineers actually learn the skills that they need to learn? and well, honestly

00:07:56.490 --> 00:08:02.029
Everybody's different some people set aside a certain amount of time every week to learn stuff, other people

00:08:02.250 --> 00:08:05.539
Take time off to do it and learn it on weekends

00:08:05.849 --> 00:08:06.949
but for me

00:08:06.949 --> 00:08:10.218
I am a very like Purpose Driven learner

00:08:10.500 --> 00:08:14.959
In a sense that I learned the best when there is a bigger goal that I want to achieve

00:08:15.419 --> 00:08:19.218
So that I can do some purposeful learning

00:08:19.529 --> 00:08:23.629
In order to build the skills to get to where I want to go

00:08:23.880 --> 00:08:29.359
So when it comes to software if there's like an app that I want to build or feature idea that I have

00:08:29.550 --> 00:08:35.089
Then usually that will kind of be my bigger goal and I will go through tutorials read books watch videos

00:08:35.399 --> 00:08:40.458
To figure out what skills and things I need to learn in order to get that job done

00:08:40.580 --> 00:08:45.060
And sometimes that job is literally just like feeding my curiosity, the other day

00:08:45.080 --> 00:08:47.260
I was like "What the heck is Blockchain?!"

00:08:47.260 --> 00:08:50.600
and then I watched some videos on it and I feel like I have a better grasp on what it is

00:08:50.600 --> 00:08:53.300
and now when people talk about blockchain, I'm like

00:08:53.870 --> 00:08:58.099
"I know what that is now!" and as I mentioned before as a person

00:08:58.290 --> 00:09:02.599
I'm always thinking about "Who am I? Where do I want to go?"

00:09:03.240 --> 00:09:06.379
"What are my current circumstances, where can I go?"

00:09:06.710 --> 00:09:12.710
"What skills do I need to get there?" So that I make the best use of my time here on this earth in this body

00:09:13.470 --> 00:09:15.470
Not existential at all

00:09:15.480 --> 00:09:19.129
And I mean it's also because as a person it's really hard to define exactly

00:09:19.260 --> 00:09:22.760
what you want to be and who you want to be because

00:09:23.360 --> 00:09:30.820
these days there's really no one right way to do life and it's just full myriad of

00:09:30.840 --> 00:09:35.300
Experiences that you wouldn't have predicted would happen at all

00:09:35.370 --> 00:09:39.050
So I guess what I'm trying to say is I like to keep it open ended and instead

00:09:39.050 --> 00:09:44.959
I really just let my curiosity fuel my learning and I don't know how to trigger curiosity

00:09:44.960 --> 00:09:48.199
I just... I just get curious and then I'm just like I'm gonna learn it

00:09:48.930 --> 00:09:53.180
So no tips there, so I'm not the type to like schedule time to learn stuff

00:09:53.180 --> 00:09:56.299
I don't set aside X amount of hours to learn about this new

00:09:56.820 --> 00:09:58.320
framework or anything

00:09:58.320 --> 00:10:06.080
I will maybe if I'm very curious about that framework find a project that will help me use that

00:10:06.330 --> 00:10:07.740
project basically

00:10:07.740 --> 00:10:12.019
but yeah having a purpose helps me stay motivated in learning stuff and

00:10:12.420 --> 00:10:17.539
it means a lot of failing and a lot of hard stuff along the way to figure out what I need to do and

00:10:17.910 --> 00:10:21.529
accepting the fact that I suck at a lot of stuff, but I mean

00:10:21.690 --> 00:10:27.530
That's how you learn and that is ultimately I feel like how you become a lifelong learner

00:10:27.530 --> 00:10:30.530
I mean it really it's a little it's kind of a lesson on humility

00:10:30.530 --> 00:10:35.869
I think and the best part is that the Internet has opened up so many new ways of learning and

00:10:36.240 --> 00:10:39.720
Consuming content because of all the amazing content creators out there.

00:10:39.720 --> 00:10:42.200
I personally learned the best from video content

